WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:19.532
So one of the questions that I get asked all the time

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.166
is when you're out in public, when you're in the real world,

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:25.166
when you're having dinner with friends, and you're meeting your neighbors or relatives,

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:30.032
what do you do if evolution comes up as a topic of conversation?

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:32.532
Or if somebody says, "I don't believe in evolution," or

00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.332
"I don't think they should teach it in the schools," or

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.532
"It's just a theory."

00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:40.066
Well, when you're right up front, this is a really good way to alienate people

00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:42.032
and get them upset with you.

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:44.932
But, I think there are much better ways to handle disagreement.

00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:50.099
And, one of the first things that I try to do when someone brings that point of view up

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.132
is politely indicate that I disagree with them,

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:53.899
so they understand what's going to follow.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.232
But then I try to do something that I think all of you,

00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:00.032
at this stage in your careers, can do very effectively.

00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:02.666
And that is, let them know how science works.

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:06.232
There's a stereotype out in the real world.

00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:09.232
There's a stereotype that science is kind of like a closed community...

00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:11.566
a real tight-knit fraternity or sorority,

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:16.666
where getting ahead depends on exactly what your superiors want,

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:18.966
and always kowtowing up to the big people -

00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:21.399
the National Academy people, department chairs, stuff like that.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:28.032
The reality, as I'm sure all of you know, is that science works in an entirely different way.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:32.199
The worst thing anybody could hope for, embarking on a research project,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.599
are results that simply confirm existing ideas. That's boring.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.932
What we all lay awake at night, dreaming about, is the revolutionary discovery --

00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:44.099
the one that's going to upset the apple cart...

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.866
the one that's going to destroy the common theory...

00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:49.332
because that's how you can make your reputation.

00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:53.099
Nobody ever got famous by confirming other's ideas.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.166
They got famous by coming up with revolutionary results

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:58.766
and hatching new ideas of their own.

00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:01.532
Emphasize that that's how science works.

00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:06.299
The same dynamic -- trust me -- works in the field of evolutionary biology,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.532
at the genetics and the molecular level.

00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:14.999
The way which people get ahead is by testing, by looking, by probing the theory

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.166
-- any theory -- for weaknesses, and that counts in evolution as well.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:24.166
And what I first try to do is let people know that science is contentious.

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:27.399
That the very dynamic of science -- the competitive nature of science --

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:32.366
guarantees that if evolution was a uniquely weak or shaky theory

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:33.499
with no evidence behind it,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.832
people would be standing in line -- scientists standing in line -- to knock it off its pedestal.

00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:39.132
They're not.

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:44.799
I can't think of a single theory in the life sciences that has been tested and probed

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.599
more thoroughly than evolution. It's a 152 years

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.166
after the publication of The Origin of Species,

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:53.232
and it's still standing. That's a darn good theory.

00:02:53.233 --> 00:02:57.466
Second point that I try to make is what a theory in science means.

00:02:57.467 --> 00:03:01.299
To an average person, theory is just a guess or a hunch.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.199
Like, I have a theory as to why the San Francisco Giants won the pennant last year.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.532
Or, I have a theory as to why this particular politician's ideas are good or bad or indifferent.

00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:14.766
That's not what we mean by theory in science.

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:18.532
Many people think that fact is something you're certain about

00:03:18.533 --> 00:03:20.232
and theory is something shaky.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:25.666
The reality is that theory is a higher level of understanding than fact,

00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:26.632
and here's the reason why.

00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:30.832
Facts are simply repeatable, isolated observations.

00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:34.566
I drop this ball, it falls down. The sun comes up in the morning.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:37.499
The wavelength of visible light is the following.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.266
These are all facts.

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:42.899
What theories do is to take collections of isolated facts

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.766
and put them together into a single, explanatory framework.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:53.766
For example, the atomic theory of matter is the theory that literally takes

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:58.332
hundreds of thousands of facts -- observations -- about the nature of matter

00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:01.832
and ties them all together into an explanatory theory.

00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:09.099
None of us in education ever expects a course in atomic theory ten years from now

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.166
to be called a course in atomic facts.

00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:14.466
As if atoms were something we weren't sure about.

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:20.666
Theories in science never become facts. Theories in science explain facts.

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:25.132
And the reality is, that although people raise a lot of arguments against evolution,

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:31.599
there isn't a single experimental fact or observation that has appeared in the last 150 years

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.732
that contradicts the broad outlines of the theory of evolution.

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:39.432
Then the last thing that I try to do is really very simple.

00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:44.599
And that is to say that science is an enterprise

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.832
in which we try to find out how the natural world works.

00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:52.432
It's not an enterprise that takes on religion in a hostile way.

00:04:52.433 --> 00:04:56.199
And, in fact, many of the greatest scientists in all history have,

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.499
in fact, been deeply religious.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.466
And we can start with Galileo and Newton, but in more recent times,

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:05.299
Gregor Mendel, the founder of the modern science of genetics.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.799
He was a priest. As it turns out, Theodosius Dobzhansky... I know it's a big name,

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.832
but he's probably the greatest evolutionary geneticist of the 20th century.

00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:16.532
He was a devout person as well.

00:05:16.533 --> 00:05:19.932
Frances Collins, a terrific cell and molecular biologist

00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:21.999
who heads the National Institutes of Health

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.599
is an evangelical Christian.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.566
All of the people that I just mentioned -- the recent people --

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:31.632
are, in fact, strong supporters and contributors to the science of evolution.

00:05:31.633 --> 00:05:35.432
Now, people can look at evolution and draw a variety of conclusions

00:05:35.433 --> 00:05:37.366
as to how it fits the religious faith.

00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:41.499
But, evolutionary science itself, like all of science, really,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.666
is neutral on issues of religion.

00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:47.499
There are ways to understand evolution within a religious context,

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.966
and I've actually written about those in a couple of books and many articles.

00:05:50.967 --> 00:05:52.399
And other scientists have too.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.566
And, finally, and here's the most important thing.

00:05:55.567 --> 00:06:00.532
When someone expresses disagreement on a point this fundamental,

00:06:00.533 --> 00:06:03.799
I think what you have to do is engage them,

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.399
rather than argue with them.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.066
And by engage, I mean, engage them as you would a colleague.

00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:11.532
If you and your colleague are working on the same problem,

00:06:11.533 --> 00:06:13.499
and you have slightly different ideas about it,

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.666
do you call each other names? Maybe, in some cases, in some fields you do.

00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:20.399
But, what you do ideally is you lay out your data.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.166
You show each other your gels, your sequences, your atomic level structures.

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:30.099
And from this airing of data, the other person begins to see

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.266
that you're a person of good will.

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:34.632
That you're just interested in solving the same problem that they are.

00:06:34.633 --> 00:06:38.266
I have found that technique -- in effect, trying to take people in

00:06:38.267 --> 00:06:40.066
to the scientific community...

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:44.866
saying, "Let me show you why we think this is so," to be enormously effective.

00:06:44.867 --> 00:06:49.666
Now, I'll guarantee you, you're not going to be familiar with every argument

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:51.832
that comes out against the theory of evolution.

00:06:51.833 --> 00:06:55.899
Some of your colleagues will. You might want to run those arguments down.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:59.632
You might want to go to websites to try to find answers to these arguments.

00:06:59.633 --> 00:07:08.666
There's a really good website called www.talkorigins.org

00:07:08.667 --> 00:07:11.199
in which all the anti-evolution arguments that I have ever heard of

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.766
are answered in a very definitive way.

00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:15.366
So there are resources out there for you.

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:19.599
But, I think the most important thing that any of us can do as a scientist

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.899
or as an apprentice scientist is to let people know why we went into science.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.132
It wasn't because we have a secular, humanist axe to grind.

00:07:28.133 --> 00:07:29.566
It's not because we want to destroy God.

00:07:29.567 --> 00:07:34.132
It's because we really, really want to understand how the world works.

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:37.366
And when people see that sincere motivation...

00:07:37.367 --> 00:07:40.599
when they see the twinkle in your eye, you're going to win them over.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:44.066
They might be a little baffled by some of the science, but once they see,

00:07:44.067 --> 00:07:49.899
as I say, that gleam in your eye -- the glint, the excitement of scientific discovery --

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:53.932
they're going to drop their suspicions of science, and they're going to appreciate you

00:07:53.933 --> 00:07:56.832
as just another person working in this great enterprise.

00:07:56.833 --> 01:00:00.000
Lots of luck with that.

